-- title:
--   Edge-weighted trees and path weights
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. Let $\mathcal T$ be an ordered tree.
--
--   1. **Weighted tree.** Positive weights $w_k(c)$ on the nodes $c$ of level $k+1$, with
--   $$
--   \sum_{c:\ \mathrm{parent}(c)=p}w_k(c)=1
--   $$
--   for every parent $p$.
--   2. **Path weight.** $\pi_0=1$ at the root and $\pi_{k+1}(c)=\pi_k(\mathrm{parent}(c))\,w_k(c)$, the product of the edge weights along the path from the root. Path weights sum to $1$ on every level and to $\pi(v)$ over the descendants of any node $v$.
